00:00:00.030 --> 00:00:01.640 align:start position:0%
 
Germany's Ursula funder line has  addressed the European Parliament in  Strasbourg as she winds up her bid to  become the first woman president of the  European Commission it was her last  chance to make her case EU lawmakers  still on the fence about her nomination  Thunder lion needs to secure a majority  in a secret confirmation ballot being  held later today in her speech she  sought to allay concerns of lawmakers  who have criticized her for being overly  vague laying out a number of ambitious  policy points on topics including trade  gender equality migration and brexit  there was also a big appeal to the green  lobby with funder Lyon pledging a green  deal for Europe in her first 100 days  achieving her goals on climate change  she said would require a new sense of  unity we must first rediscover our unity  if we are united on the inside  nobody will divide us from the outside  if we close the gaps between us we can  turn tomorrow's challenges in tomorrow's  opportunities honourable members our  most pressing challenge is keeping our  planet healthy this is the greatest  responsibility and opportunity of our  times  let's bring in now DWS chief political  correspondent Melinda crane who joins us  here in the studio and our Brussels  bureau chief max Hoffman is standing by  at the European Parliament in Strasbourg  max I'll turn to you first because her  nomination has been controversial  especially among parliament deputies  this was really her last chance to  convinced them do you think that she did  because I mean we just heard from the  Greens and they say we're still not back  in her it seems though that the stars  are aligning in favor of was Olaf on the  line we talked to a couple of MEP s here  in the meantime and even the Greens that  were the first to say we are not going  to vote for lose enough on the line  after meeting her last week said this  was a pretty good speech we talked to  one representative of the Belgian Greens  for example  and I asked her on a scale of one to ten  ten being the best score what do you  give Oz Olaf on the line for that  speeches she said a seven or eight so  she really managed to cater towards the  greens whether this really changes their  overall decision not to vote for her  that's something they're gonna have to  hash out this afternoon when the  political groups meet but if even the  greens are a little shaky now on their  position it means that she probably  swayed some votes among the Social  Democrats it probably also means that  the EPP is even more firmly behind her  than before EP P being the Conservatives  her own party  so most people we talked to here seem to  think that she's going to make it okay  and no matter which way you cut it  though at the end of the day this is a  historic vote this is the first time  that a woman has gone up for a president  of the European Commission she addressed  that issue let's have a listen and then  I'll get your reaction thereafter I will  ensure full gender equality in my  college of commissioners  if member states do not propose enough  female commissioners I will not hesitate  to ask for new names since 1958 there  have been 183 commissioners only 35 were  women that is less less than 20%  we represent half of our population we  want our fair share quoting some  embarrassing statistics there Melinda is  this gender card working for her or  against her you think we'll know this  evening but I think it could very well  work for her because the party groups  that she most needs to convince she has  the Conservatives behind her anyway and  they like by the way the fact that she  she's a woman but she's also a mother of  seven children that that plays well with  conservatives so the other party groups  that she needs to convince our Social  Democrats and also members of the  Liberal Party that's the market friendly  party and I think on both counts there  are a lot of MEP s members of European  Parliament who do support stronger roles  for women so I think she is possibly  likely to ratchet up a little bit in  their estimation on the basis of that  really quite clear statement right at  the outset of her speech max tell us  because one of the big issues has also  been the fact that she is not the  so-called lead candidate the the Schmitz  encana dot just walk us through the  controversy there and how big of a  barrier this could be to her election  well as you know the lead candidate  principle was invented five years ago  and the idea behind it was that only one  of those lead candidates of the  different political groups here in their  European Parliament would be eligible to  become Commission president afterwards  this worked quite well five years ago  because the two biggest groups back then  the Social Democrats and the  Conservatives were in the same page and  said okay the European People's Party  the conservative had the largest share  of their vote and jean-claude Juncker  was their lead candidate and therefore  he will be lected among parliamentarians  we have a majority and this will work so  the leaders really didn't have another  choice but to put acknowledged this and  say ok we're on board with it if they  didn't want to risk a huge institutional  fight this time the Parliament did not  have a majority behind one of those  leaked candidates neither behind the  lead candidate of the Conservatives who  was monthly vibha neither behind  northern candidate of the Social  Democrats who was fenced Timmerman's and  that's why leaders took over they took  control of the process and now the  European Parliament is complaining about  what laughs underline because she was  not on the campaign trail because she  was not one of the visually candidates  and because effectively it takes away  some of the power that the European  Parliament fought for and Ashley got  five years ago Melinda how was her  candidacy playing back at home in  Germany mixed at least heretofore we  don't know about the response that  German citizens will have had to hearing  that I must say unusually strong speech  coming from ursula fund ella and she's  not known as a fiery speaker but there  was quite a bit of emotional pathos at  the end of that speech so last week a  survey showed that only about one in  three Germans felt that she would make a  good president of the European  Commission that depended a bit on party  affiliation those who were affiliated I  would either with the Conservative Party  or the free market oriented FDP Liberal  Party they a slight majority said they  did think she'd make a good Commission  president but on the whole not a lot of  resounding support that's partly because  she's had a tough job here as defense  minister instituted reforms that haven't  yet  played out there has been some criticism  of her leadership and in some ways her  image here at home is less bright than  it is in many other European countries  okay  max how will this vote now play out for  the rest of the day and do European  leaders have another surprise candidate  perhaps up their sleeves if Parliament  votes are Silla founder lying down right  now she's still being quizzed in the  plenary room of the European Parliament  here this will go on until approximately  twelve o'clock local time so another  hour or so because many of the  parliamentarians still have questions  there's only so many things you can talk  about in about 40 minutes speech so  there'll be more specific questions  she's being grilled but the the big  politician the top shots the political  group leaders have already asked their  questions then in the afternoon the  different political groups will meet to  discuss how they're going to act it  doesn't necessarily mean that all the  members of those political groups will  follow the recommendation made by the  group take the social democrats for  example they will most likely recommend  voting for azul upon the lion but the  German delegation of that group the SPD  was very was very adamant about not  voting for her by the way something that  raised some eyebrows around here also  among Social Democrats from other  European countries and then this evening  at 6 o'clock we have the secret ballot  we have the boat it'll take a while to  count all those votes and we do expect  an official result by 7:30 we do expect  her to win maybe by a small margin but  you can't be sure because it's a secret  ballot and if she loses we're back to  square one and the leaders will have to  suggest someone else over the summer  okay so a very jam-packed day today the  stakes are high certainly a historic day  for the European Union max Holtzman in  Brussels Melinda Crain here in the  studio thank you so much to both of you

